cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "BORDER OPTICS: Evaluating a Place-Based Border Programme from Space: Evidence and Indicator Limits from India's Vibrant Villages Programme"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
abstract: >-
  An independent satellite assessment of India's Vibrant Villages Programme
  (VVP-I). It compares 220 geocoded priority villages in Arunachal Pradesh
  and Uttarakhand with 628 non-priority villages in the same 13 districts,
  using Sentinel-2 NDBI, VIIRS night lights, Sentinel-1 backscatter and
  Dynamic World built-up probability in a within-district
  difference-in-differences design (2021-2025) with small-cluster inference.
  NDBI, night lights and radar show no difference between the groups, but a
  detectability analysis shows that buffer-mean NDBI could not have detected
  even a doubling of a typical village's buildings. Dynamic World shows a small
  positive difference in the summer window, roughly 1,900 square metres of
  built-up surface per village; the full-year estimate is not significant. A pooled association
  between change and border distance disappears or reverses within districts.
  The repository includes all code, data, figures, a Streamlit dashboard and a
  full development log.
keywords:
  - Vibrant Villages Programme
  - policy evaluation
